      "text": "To compute \\(\\cos 150^\\circ\\), we can use the angle addition formula for cosine. Notice that \\(150^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 30^\\circ\\). Therefore, we can express \\(\\cos 150^\\circ\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = \\cos (180^\\circ - 30^\\circ)\n\\]\n\nWe know from trigonometric identities that \\(\\cos (180^\\circ - \\theta) = -\\cos \\theta\\). Applying this identity, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = \\cos (180^\\circ - 30^\\circ) = -\\cos 30^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThe value of \\(\\cos 30^\\circ\\) is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Therefore,\n\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = -\\cos 30^\\circ = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
